  "limitations": [],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This empirical study investigates individual differences in susceptibility to auditory distraction during a serial recall task. It challenges traditional working memory accounts, proposing instead that distraction is linked to the efficacy of obligatory perceptual organization of auditory sequences. Key findings include that while deliberate sequence processing (recoding) performance was positively related to serial recall performance, it did not predict susceptibility to distraction. Conversely, performance on a global pattern-matching task (reflecting preattentive auditory processing) positively predicted susceptibility to distraction, suggesting that better perceptual organization of irrelevant sound leads to greater interference. The study found no explicit limitations, but its findings imply that the functional character of auditory distraction differs from that related to executive control.",
